{"name":"Peaches à la Crème","hex":"#ffa361","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"peaches","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of peach"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of peach"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peaches"},{"term":"crème","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Synonym of cream."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very sugary, fluffy white cream derivative or creamlike analogue.","Cream."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cr%C3%A8me"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glossary of French words and expressions in English","blocks":[{"text":"Many words in the English language are of French origin (\"gallicism\"s). Most of them came from the Anglo-Norman spoken by the upper classes in England for several hundred years after the Norman Conquest, before the language settled into what became Modern English. English words of French origin, such as art, competition, force, money, and table are pronounced according to English rules of phonology, rather than French, and English speakers commonly use them without any awareness of their French origin."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glossary_of_French_words_and_expressions_in_English"},{"title":"Peach","blocks":[{"text":"The peach is a deciduous tree that bears edible juicy fruits with various characteristics. Most are simply called peaches, while the glossy-skinned, non-fuzzy varieties are called nectarines. Though from the same species, they are regarded commercially as different fruits."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg/330px-Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach"},{"title":"À","blocks":[{"text":"À, known as A-grave, is a Latin-script character composed of the letter A and a grave accent. It is found in the Catalan, Emilian-Romagnol, French, Italian, Maltese, Occitan, Portuguese, Sardinian, Scottish Gaelic, Vietnamese, and Welsh languages consisting of the letter A of the ISO basic Latin alphabet and a grave accent. À is also used in Pinyin transliteration. In most languages, it represents the vowel a. This letter is also a letter in Taos to indicate a mid tone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Latin_letter_A_with_grave.svg/330px-Latin_letter_A_with_grave.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%80"},{"title":"A","blocks":[{"text":"A is the first letter and the first vowel letter of the Latin alphabet, used in the modern English alphabet, and others worldwide. Its name in English is a, plural aes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Latin_letter_A.svg/330px-Latin_letter_A.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Aperitif_for_five.jpg/500px-Aperitif_for_five.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Aperitif_for_five.jpg/1280px-Aperitif_for_five.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Aperitif_for_five.jpg/500px-Aperitif_for_five.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Apéritifs with amuse-gueules","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAperitif_for_five.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Striding_Edge.jpg/500px-Striding_Edge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Striding_E